<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lenape</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Delaware people, Lenni Lenape</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lenape, also called the Delaware, are an Algonquian-speaking Indigenous people whose homeland covered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delaware-river">Delaware River</a> valley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a> and the lower Hudson valley, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a>. They traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and Swedish colonists in the 17th century and made treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-penn">William Penn</a>, but lost most of their land, notably through the Walking Purchase of 1737. Pushed westward, most of their descendants now live in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lenape.jpg" alt="Lenape" width="320" height="484" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nikater, 1 Feb 2007 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lenape01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Lenni Lenape combines two autonyms, Lenni, meaning genuine or original, and Lenape, meaning real person or original person, and some Lenape prefer simply Lenape. The name Delaware came from the English colonists, who named the river after Lord De La Warr, first governor of the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, and applied it to the people who lived there. When Europeans first met them, the Lenape were a loose association of closely related peoples who spoke similar languages and shared family bonds in Lenapehoking, their country, which spanned what is now eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>, Lower New York Bay and eastern Delaware.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first recorded contact was in 1524, when Giovanni da Verrazzano's ship entered Lower New York Bay and local people came out in canoes. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> set up Fort Nassau in the 1620s, the Lenape restricted Dutch settlement until the 1660s to no further than Pavonia on the Hudson. Dutch and Lenape relations centred on trade. Europeans often tried to contract for land with chiefs, mistaking Lenape culture for that of neighbours such as the Haudenosaunee, whereas in Lenape society clan and family controlled property, and kinship terms meant different things to each side. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-penn">William Penn</a> first met the Lenape in 1682.</p><p>After Penn's death in 1718 his heirs and their agents sought ways to sell Lenape land, which led to the Walking Purchase. In the 1730s colonial administrators produced a draft deed dating to the 1680s, which the Penns presented as legitimate, but Lenape leaders in the lower Delaware refused to accept it. In the last decades of the 18th century settlers and the American Revolutionary War displaced most Lenape north and west, and in the 1860s the federal government moved most of those still in the east to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a>. Groups that fled to what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> remain there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Lenape never formed a political unity. A person identified first with family, clan and village, then with neighbouring villages, then with speakers of the same dialect and finally with those whose languages were mutually understood, including the Nanticoke. The Unami and Munsee languages belong to the Eastern Algonquian group and are largely mutually intelligible.</p><p>Women grew the Three Sisters, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, beans and squash, while men hunted and fished, and the Lenape used fire to manage land. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> settlers and Swedes admired their clothing, such as women's skirts painted or decorated with porcupine quills. Federally recognised Lenape communities today are in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a>, and three First Nations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> are recognised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lenape” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Covenant Chain, the alliance made at Albany in 1677 with Governor Edmund Andros of New York and later extended to other colonies, they became the principal Native partner of the English and claimed authority over many dependent peoples, including the <b>Delaware</b> and remnant groups of the Susquehannock.</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Company policy required land to be bought from the Indians, but the Dutch took their gifts of sewant and goods as exclusive title while the <b>Lenape</b> bands meant to share, not leave, and the misunderstanding bred violence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-netherland">New Netherland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>West Jersey and Pennsylvania were established by William Penn in 1676 and 1682, and he signed a peace treaty with Tamanend, leader of the <b>Lenape</b>; the peace endured almost a century, until the Penn's Creek Massacre of 1755.</blockquote><a href="/movement/quakers">Quakers · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Lenape</b> and Wyandot warriors killed American settlers near present-day Stockport, Ohio, in the Big Bottom massacre.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-2">2 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>He soon bought the island from the <b>Lenape</b> for goods worth 60 guilders, and New Amsterdam became its capital.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-4">4 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It was essentially a treaty of military alliance between the <b>Lenape</b> Nation and the United States.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-17">17 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
